# ============================================================
# Error Handling Standards
# ============================================================

# Philosophy

Errors are part of the API contract.

Every failure should be

predictable

consistent

typed

recoverable where appropriate.

Unexpected exceptions should never leak to clients.

---

# Exception Flow

Client

↓

Router

↓

Use Case

↓

Repository

↓

Database

Errors travel upward

while becoming

more abstract.

At the presentation boundary,

**global exception handlers**

convert `ApplicationException` subclasses

into HTTP responses with the standard error envelope.

Routers do not build error JSON.

Never expose infrastructure exceptions.

---

# Global Exception Handlers

All HTTP error translation is centralized.

Location

```text
api/app/core/exception_handlers.py
```

Registration occurs in `create_app()` in `main.py` (after `FastAPI(...)` is created).

Handlers MUST

- map each `ApplicationException` subclass to one HTTP status (see Recommended Mapping)
- build responses using the envelope in `10-response-format.mdc`
- log unexpected failures; log business failures at appropriate severity
- never expose stack traces, SQL, or internal details to clients

Handlers MAY register for

- project `ApplicationException` types
- `fastapi.exceptions.RequestValidationError`
- `pydantic.ValidationError` (when raised outside FastAPI request validation)
- a final catch-all for unexpected `Exception` → 500

FastAPI `RequestValidationError` uses **422** and the same error envelope shape.

Routers MUST NOT

- wrap use cases in `try/except` for application exceptions
- return manual `JSONResponse` error bodies
- duplicate handler logic per endpoint

