# Log Context vs Details

`details` is part of the API contract — it is serialized to the client via
`_envelope_json` in `exception_handlers.py`. Never put internal-only
information there (ids not meant to be inferred, driver text, entity state).

`ApplicationException.log_context` is the sibling field for that: a
`dict[str, Any]` merged into the global handler's log line
(`application_exception_handler`) but never serialized to a response. Use it
when a failure should carry diagnostic fields (e.g. `student_id`, `exam_id`)
without exposing them to the client.

Good

```python
raise ConflictException(
    message="Report already published",
    code="REPORT_ALREADY_PUBLISHED",
    log_context={"report_id": report_id},
)
```

Bad

```python
raise ConflictException(
    message="Report already published",
    code="REPORT_ALREADY_PUBLISHED",
    details={"report_id": report_id},  # leaks an internal id to the client
)
```

Fields named `code`, `message`, `status_code`, or `exc_info` inside
`log_context` are ignored — the handler always sets those itself.

