# Exception Chaining

Preserve root cause.

Use exception chaining.

Example

raise PersistenceException(...) from exc

Never discard

original exceptions.

`application_exception_handler` merges `log_context` from the full
`__cause__` chain and always logs compact `cause_type`/`cause_message`
(the immediate cause) plus `root_cause_type`/`root_cause_message` (the
innermost cause in the chain, when it differs from the immediate one) —
e.g. for `ServiceUnavailableException -> PersistenceException -> the raw
driver error`, `cause_*` is the curated boundary message and `root_cause_*`
is the actual driver failure. Both are truncated/sanitized single-line
strings, safe for production logs. Full tracebacks (`exc_info`) are
attached only when `settings.debug` is true — `LOG_LEVEL` controls which
*events* are emitted, not traceback verbosity; the two are independent
knobs. Root cause detail never enters `details` or the client response.
This only works if the chain is preserved — never wrap a caught failure
without `from exc`.

`cause_message`/`root_cause_message` are truncated (`_sanitize_cause_message`)
and reflect only what the exception's own `str()` happens to contain. If a
repository's underlying error message wouldn't be specific or stable enough
on its own (or risks being truncated away), attach the important fields
explicitly via `log_context` (e.g. `log_context={"operation": "ping",
"driver": "asyncmy"}`) instead of relying on message-string parsing.

